  ],
  "services": [
    {
      "name": "postmortem",
      "tool_prefix": "postmortem_"
    }
  ],
  "tool_descriptions": {
    "postmortem_get_capabilities": "Return live data coverage counts (events, notes, attachments, alarms, PV selections, PV catalog), the data_as_of snapshot date and total, index freshness, available tools, examples, and known limitations. Call this first. It also returns abbreviations: the device-name table for CM, LLRF, HPRF, KSU, IC, MIS, PSI, MPS, BLM, BPM, DCCT, TFS, FOFB, BCL01/BCL03, BPR01, BPS01, and channel prefixes. Read it before interpreting device tags. The DB is refreshed every few months, so quote the data_as_of date whenever you report totals.",
    "postmortem_search_events": "Search beam-dump events by natural-language query and/or structured filters. Filters: year, year_from/year_to, year_min/year_max, date (YYYY-MM-DD), subsystem (KSU1-3/CM1-3/CRG/MPS...), fault_type (string or list), fault_types (list), event_id, pv (matches event_alarms/event_pv_selection PV names), beam_time (partial beam-time label such as '2026-11차'), has_waveform (bool), hide_ms (default true; excludes Machine Study beam times such as 2026-01MS), sort ('relevance'|'recent'), limit, offset. fault_type/fault_types match the manual user_fault_type first, then the automatic fault_type. Questions containing 최근/요즘/최신 automatically use sort='recent'. Returns response_type='event_list' with items[]; each item has id, event_id, timestamp, fault_type (auto), auto_fault_type, effective_fault_type (manual override first), case_id (auto), effective_case_id (manual override first), classification_note (set only when manual differs from auto), user_fault_type, notes, alarm_count, pv_selection_count, has_waveform, url, citation. Use aggregate for counts.",
    "postmortem_get_event": "Return one event's full context: notes, fault classification (effective_fault_type applies the manual override first), recorded alarms, manual PV selections, catalog mappings, attachments, similar events, waveform availability. Returns response_type='event_detail' with event, alarms[], pv_selection[], catalog[], attachments[], similar[], waveform, caveats. An empty similar[] means the DB has no event_links for this event, not an API error. Set include_images=true (or waveform_image=true) to append MCP Image blocks for the pre-rendered forward/cavity/reflect JPEGs; image_scope=all|forward|cavity|reflect or a comma list. JSON stays in content[0].text. image records include event_id, visible_channels, and panel caption.",
    "postmortem_explain_event": "Preferred single-event explanation tool. Returns a deterministic evidence bundle: event, manual-vs-auto classification, primary_interlock, quench unit/source, per-cavity sequence, tau_reliable, per-cavity tau, root_cause from notes, mechanism steps, warnings, claim_checks, required_disclosures, and image panel metadata. Use this before writing any causal explanation. The physics.class is explicitly marked observational; do not use it as a root-cause label. Set include_images=true to append MCP Image blocks.",
    "postmortem_analyze_cohort": "Summarize a cohort of waveform events by class, quench_unit, first interlock, fault_family, year range, tau_reliable, beam delay range, or waveform availability. Returns counts/distributions, median/min/max beam delay, and representative examples sorted by delay. Use this for whole-dataset patterns; then call postmortem_explain_event for examples. Do not use vector search for counts.",
    "postmortem_find_examples": "Return curated teaching examples by topic. Topics include cm_single_fault (CM ARC/flowmeter, not quench), ksu_single_station, and quench_propagation (with explicit cautions). Each example includes why_clean, caution, summary, warnings, and waveform image metadata. Use this before choosing an event as a canonical example.",
    "postmortem_search_notes": "Hybrid lexical + dense retrieval over operator event notes. Use it to find similar wording/cases, not for counts, timing, or causal proof. Returns event_id, timestamp, fault_type, notes, dense/lexical scores, citation, and url. Falls back to lexical search if onnxruntime/model files are unavailable.",
    "postmortem_route_question": "Rule-based routing hint for a natural-language question. Returns recommended tools, glossary topics, suggested filters, cautions, and a reminder that the route is not an answer. Use it when unsure which MCP tools to call first.",
    "postmortem_aggregate_events": "Count events grouped by one or more fields. group_by accepts year, month (YYYY-MM), fault_type, case_id, auto_case_id, auto_fault_type, user_fault_type, beam_time, or a comma-separated composite such as 'year,beam_time'. case_id follows the manual classification first; auto_case_id is the automatic reference. fault_type grouping uses the manual user_fault_type first, then the automatic fault_type. Filters: year, year_from, year_to, subsystem, fault_type (string or list), fault_types (list), text, pv, beam_time. hide_ms (default true) excludes Machine Study periods. include_percent defaults to true. Rows carry key, count, percent; copy them verbatim and never estimate or recompute. Returns response_type='aggregate' with group_by[], total, sum_of_counts, rows:[{key, keys, count, percent?}], and data_as_of (latest event timestamp, DB total, and an 'as of YYYY-MM-DD, total N events' statement). Quote the data_as_of date when reporting totals. Use this for counts/trends; do not use vector search for counts.",
    "postmortem_similar_events": "Return events linked to the given event by the DB event_links similarity graph. Returns response_type='event_list' with event_id, returned, items[]; each item has both id and event_id. Empty items means no links exist in the DB. This is not a vector similarity score.",
    "postmortem_search_alarm_catalog": "Search the 524-entry alarm PV catalog by PV name, description, or graph PV. Returns response_type='pv_list' with items[]; each item has pv, description, subsystem, graph_pv, graph_description, severity, citation. Results are catalog mappings, not evidence that an alarm occurred in an event.",
    "postmortem_get_alarm_status": "Return the live alarm monitor status, last-state counts, and recent transitions. Returns response_type='alarm_status' with monitor, last_state_counts, last_state_updated, recent_transitions, catalog, caveats. Check last_heartbeat and last_state_updated before calling this current.",
    "postmortem_resolve_event": "Resolve a date (YYYY-MM-DD) and optional subsystem to candidate event IDs. Returns response_type='event_list' with returned and items[] (id + event_id). If multiple events match, choose one and call postmortem_get_event.",
    "postmortem_list_fault_types": "List fault types with descriptions, severity, and event counts. Counts use the manual user_fault_type first, then the automatic fault_type. hide_ms (default true) excludes Machine Study beam times. Returns response_type='fault_type_list' with items[].",
    "postmortem_list_beam_times": "List user beam-time labels with event counts. hide_ms (default true) omits Machine Study (*MS) beam times. Returns response_type='beam_time_list' with items[].",
    "postmortem_get_pv_series": "Return an Archiver time-series summary for a PV around an event's t0. Parameters: event_id, pv, pre=60, post=10, include_points=false. Supports KSU/CRM/CRG/LRF/G00 PVs. Returns response_type='pv_series' with available, quality, summary:{count,min,max,median,peak_value,peak_rel_sec,max_deviation_pct,resolution_s}, and optional points[].",
    "postmortem_analyze_waveform": "Return numeric waveform features for one event from the static feature store: t0, beam-current fall timing, CM1/2/3 cavity sag, reflect rise, forward trip, digital interlock transitions, quench unit, and data caveats. The 'sequence' block adds cavity 90/50/10% crossing times, the cavity-to-beam delay, the 3-cavity collapse spread, and a sequence pattern (rf_trip, beam_first_rf_trip, beam_only, single_cavity_quench, mixed). The pattern is descriptive; root cause is not assigned. The 'physics' block adds a deterministic textbook-grounded interpretation with PLS-II parameter checks, citations, cause_candidates (possible causes, not a root-cause assignment), and next_logs_ko. A cavity probe dip-and-rise is an oscillation, not necessarily 'recovery'. Covers 313/866 events (2023+, parquet only). Values are computed by code, not an LLM. Returns response_type='waveform_analysis'. Set generate_plot=true (or include_images=true) to append MCP Image blocks for the pre-rendered narrow-scope JPEGs; image_scope=all|forward|cavity|reflect. JSON stays in content[0].text. Use postmortem_find_similar_waveforms to compare waveforms; use postmortem_get_event for notes/alarms.",
    "postmortem_find_similar_waveforms": "Return the top_k events whose waveform feature vector is closest (cosine) to the given event. Model waveform_manual_v1 is a 78-dim hand-crafted vector (sag/reflect/forward/beam-fall/digital), not a learned embedding. Returns response_type='waveform_similarity' with target and items[] (event_id, similarity, signature, delta_vs_target, citation). Only events with parquet are searchable. This is not the DB event_links graph.",
    "postmortem_search_reports": "Search the indexed maintenance/fault reports (91 unique HWP/HWPX/PDF/DOCX documents from 96 attachments) by text and image captions. Hybrid FTS5 trigram + dense cosine (multilingual-e5-small, CPU only), reciprocal-rank fused. Parameters: query, event_id (optional), top_k (<=30). Returns response_type='report_search' with items[]: kind='chunk' (table/narrative text) or kind='image' (Qwen2.5-VL caption + OCR), title, event_id/event_ids, text/caption/ocr_text, citation [R:event/doc] or [R:event/doc#imgN], dense_score, fts_score. Treat VLM-transcribed numbers as pointers to the source image, not ground truth. Use postmortem_get_event for notes/alarms and postmortem_search_events for event lists.",
    "postmortem_analyze_quench": "Integrated quench analysis for one event: 100 ms waveform features (cavity sag, reflect rise, forward trip, beam fall, QUEN asserts), Archiver BCL01/BCL03 cavity temperatures and beam current around the Archiver t0, and report text/image-caption evidence. Parameters: event_id, include_pv=true, include_reports=true, include_images=false, image_scope=all|forward|cavity|reflect, pre=60, post=120. Returns response_type='quench_analysis' with event, waveform, pv.series, reports.items, highlights, caveats. PVs are ~1 Hz; waveform t0 and Archiver t0 are different clocks.",
    "postmortem_compare_quench_events": "Compare waveform features across up to 20 quench events. Parameters: event_ids (list), include_reports=false. Returns response_type='quench_comparison' with rows[]: timestamp, quench unit, t0, cavity sag/pre-state, reflect rise, forward trip, beam fall, QUEN assert, report titles. Use postmortem_analyze_quench for PV/temperature evidence; this table is fast and does not call the Archiver.",
    "postmortem_get_glossary": "Return curated background knowledge for PLS-II SRF beam-dump postmortems. This is a static, textbook-grounded reference library, not event data. Call it before interpreting waveform features, writing a causal chain, or answering any SRF/beam-physics question. DOMAIN GLOSSARY (config/domain_glossary.json), 10 addressable topics: abbreviations (device names and channel prefixes: CM, LLRF, HPRF, KSU, IC, MIS, PSI, MPS, BLM, BPM, DCCT, TFS, FOFB, BCL01/BCL03, BPR01, BPS01; read this before expanding a device tag), system_overview (ring/RF layout and operating context), interlock_hierarchy (external MIS/PSI vs internal CM/KSU trip chain), trip_sequence (standard MIS beam-dump sequence, first-interlock rule), signals (analog/digital definitions, active_low/active_high polarity), naming (actual PV/channel names), data_rules (MS exclusion, manual vs auto classification, relay delay), answer_style (required caveats and quoting rules), authority (document/data authority order), caveats (standing limitations). PHYSICS LIBRARY (config/physics_references.json), 8 topics and 31 references: cavity_figures_of_merit (Q0, QL, R/Q, beta, U, Pc, Pbeam, beta_opt), cavity_decay_after_rf_off (tau=2QL/omega; PLS-II 95-108 us natural, 24-32 us beam-loaded, midpoint 28 us), beam_loading_llrf (V_c=V_g+V_b; beam phase slip/beam loading can make the probe voltage oscillate; over-input protection limits forward to about 1.2x nominal; He vessel/rupture disk protection), mis_machine_interlock (PLC 3-layer MIS; RF Ready = MIS healthy AND PSI healthy; PSI handles radiation/access; MPS is separate; BLM is monitoring), beam_energy_loss_after_rf_trip (dE/dt=-U0/T_rev=1.32 GeV/ms; PLS-II RF acceptance 2.8% at 4.5 MV), transverse_instability (exponential growth; PLS-II TFS and FOFB; BPM limits), beam_dump_causes (empirical PLS-II taxonomy: RF single station, RF common, MPS, MIS, CONTROL/TFS/FOFB, BEAMLINE, MECHVAC, KICKER, MAGNET, PSI, LINAC, external; each class also lists cause_candidates), pls2_operating_point (3 GeV, 400 mA, 499.973 MHz, 3 cavities at 1.5 MV each = 4.5 MV, Q0/Qext, R/Q=89 ohm, RF acceptance 2.8%). The full physics document also carries one internal test note (2020-04-05 vertical test, 500 MHz CESR-B): it is measured test data, not a textbook citation, and is under 'internal_test_notes'. A physics topic returns title, summary_ko, equations[{name,expr,note}], categories[] (for beam_dump_causes), citations[{id,title,authors,year,doi,url,locator,supports}], and caveats. A domain topic returns its JSON object or entry list. Parameters: topic (optional). topic='list' returns the topic index only. Omit topic to return both documents (physics under 'physics'). An unknown topic returns the valid keys. Quote equations and parameter values from the response; do not substitute values from memory."
  },
